Zero → Production-Ready AI Engineer: 12-Month Roadmap
Who this is for: a Support Engineer (telecom / charging systems) with intermediate Python, basic GenAI, a full-time job and an MCA, aiming to interview as an AI / Applied AI / Full-Stack AI / Forward-Deployed Engineer in ~12 months at the level of a strong 2-year candidate.

Budget: 18–20 hrs/week ≈ 1,000 hours for the year. Weekly split: ~4h theory · ~6h coding practice (incl. DSA) · ~8h project · ~2h review/interview.

Mentor's framing before we start

Three things I'm going to hold you to:

Everything ships. No stage ends with "I understand it". It ends with a running thing, a test suite, and a README you could defend in an interview.
One product, three projects. Your three portfolio projects are three layers of the same product: an AI-Powered Telecom Support Operations Platform built on synthetic data. Project 1 is the core platform, Project 2 adds the knowledge assistant, Project 3 adds the agentic layer. This gives you one coherent story ("I built the thing I wished I had at work") instead of three unrelated demos, and every hour compounds.
The list gets cut. Your original list has ~80 technologies. You will go deep on ~12, get working knowledge of ~15, and only be conversant about the rest. That is what a strong 2-year engineer actually looks like.
Security rule (non-negotiable): nothing from your employer or its client appears in your portfolio. No real logs, tickets, node names, schemas, vendor-specific internals, screenshots, or customer data. You describe your domain generically ("online charging / billing support for a telecom operator") and build with synthetic data you generate yourself. Every repo gets a SYNTHETIC_DATA.md stating this. If in doubt, leave it out.

