Part 2 — The Complete 12-Month Roadmap
Each month has: focus, deliverable, DSA block, competency gate, and capstone. Months 1 is broken into weeks in Part 3; each later month gets its week-by-week breakdown when you reach it (ask me at the start of each month, with your gate results).

Phase A — Professional Backend Engineer (Months 1–4)
Month 1 — Professional Python + SQL + FastAPI foundations

Learn: Python the way a team uses it (packaging, typing, dataclasses, exceptions, logging, pytest, generators, decorators, context managers), Git workflow, SQL basics, FastAPI + Pydantic v2 + SQLAlchemy 2.0 + Alembic, Postgres in Docker.
Deliverable: Project 1 v0.1 — Ticket & Alert API with migrations, tests, Compose, synthetic data seeder. (Full spec in Part 4.)
DSA: Arrays, strings, hash maps, sets (16–18 problems).
Capstone: Part 5.
Month 2 — Production FastAPI + PostgreSQL depth

Learn: Repository/service layering, dependency injection properly, custom exception handling + RFC-7807-style error responses, request/response validation edge cases, JWT auth + refresh tokens + RBAC, middleware (request IDs, timing), pagination/filtering/sorting, OpenAPI hygiene. SQL: joins, aggregations, subqueries, CTEs, window functions, indexes, EXPLAIN ANALYZE, transactions and isolation levels, constraints, normalization.
Deliverable: Project 1 v0.5 — auth, roles (agent / senior / admin), ticket lifecycle with state machine, full-text search on tickets, audit log table, 80%+ test coverage on services.
DSA: Two pointers, sliding window, prefix sums, stack (16 problems).
Gate: Given a slow query on a 1M-row synthetic tickets table, make it fast using EXPLAIN + an index, and explain why. Debug a broken auth flow I hand you.
Capstone: "Cold whiteboard": design the ticket + alert + user schema from scratch, then implement the search endpoint with filters and pagination, no references, in 3 hours.
Month 3 — Background jobs, caching, React + TypeScript

Learn: Redis (caching patterns, TTLs, invalidation), a task queue (arq or Celery), idempotency, retries with backoff, scheduled jobs. Async FastAPI correctly (when async helps, when it hurts). React + TS essentials: components, hooks, data fetching (TanStack Query), forms, routing, auth-aware UI, a dashboard with charts.
Deliverable: Project 1 v0.9 — dashboard (ticket volume, alert trends, SLA breaches), background job that ingests synthetic alert streams and detects threshold breaches, cached aggregates, email/webhook notification stub.
DSA: Queue, linked list, binary search (16 problems).
Gate: Build a "rate-limited notification dispatcher" worker with retries and dead-letter handling, no tutorial.
Capstone: Deliberately break your own system three ways (kill Redis, corrupt a job payload, overload the API) and write the incident report with fixes.
Month 4 — Ship it: Docker, cloud, CI/CD, observability

Learn: Multi-stage Dockerfiles, Compose for dev vs. prod, environment/secrets management, GitHub Actions (lint → test → build → deploy), one cloud platform deploy (managed Postgres + Redis + container), structured JSON logging, health checks, basic metrics (Prometheus-style or platform-native), error tracking.
Deliverable: Project 1 v1.0 deployed and public, with README architecture diagram, ADRs (architecture decision records), demo credentials, synthetic data statement.
DSA: Recursion, trees, BST (16 problems).
Gate: Fresh machine → clone → docker compose up → working app in under 5 minutes. CI green. Explain every line of your Dockerfile.
Capstone: Phase A Final: in one weekend, build a small unrelated CRUD service (e.g. "on-call roster manager") from zero to deployed with tests and CI, without looking anything up except official docs. If this takes more than 12 hours, Phase A isn't done.
Phase B — LLM Application Engineer (Months 5–7)
Month 5 — LLM fundamentals + structured outputs + tool calling

Learn: Tokens, tokenizers, context windows, attention/transformer intuition (enough to explain why context is quadratic and why position matters), sampling/temperature/top-p, embeddings as geometry, why LLMs hallucinate. Engineering: structured outputs with Pydantic schemas, tool calling loops by hand (no framework), retries and validation, prompt versioning, cost + latency measurement, streaming. Build your own tiny eval harness (dataset → run → score → report).
Deliverable: Project 1 gains AI ticket classification (category, priority, sentiment via structured output) with an eval set of 200 synthetic tickets, precision/recall per class, and a cost-per-ticket number. Start Project 2 repo: document ingestion pipeline skeleton.
DSA: Heap, backtracking (16 problems).
Gate: Implement a tool-calling agent loop in raw Python (no LangChain) that answers "which tickets breached SLA yesterday?" by calling your own API. Explain every message in the transcript.
Capstone: Write the eval report for your classifier as if for a manager: what it gets wrong, why, what you'd change.
Month 6 — RAG: ingestion → retrieval

Learn: Parsing (PDF, markdown, HTML), cleaning, chunking strategies (fixed, recursive, semantic, structural) and their trade-offs, embedding models and dimensions, pgvector indexing (HNSW vs IVFFlat), hybrid search (BM25 + vector), metadata filtering, reranking (cross-encoder), context construction and citation mapping, retrieval evaluation (recall@k, MRR).
Deliverable: Project 2 v0.5 — ingest a synthetic corpus (runbooks, KB articles, past incident post-mortems you write yourself or adapt from public telecom docs), hybrid retrieval with filters (system, severity, date), reranking, a retrieval eval set with 100 questions and recall@k tracked across chunking experiments.
DSA: Graphs, BFS/DFS (16 problems).
Gate: Given a new document type, add it to the pipeline in under an hour. Show me a chunking experiment table with numbers.
Capstone: A/B two chunking strategies and two retrieval strategies; write up which won and why, with graphs.
Month 7 — RAG: generation, citations, evaluation, hallucination control, ship

Learn: Grounded generation prompts, citation formats and verification, answer-faithfulness and answer-relevance evals (LLM-as-judge with calibration against human labels), refusal when evidence is weak, conversation memory for follow-ups, query rewriting, caching embeddings and answers, cost/latency dashboards, guardrails for prompt injection via documents, auth-scoped retrieval (users only retrieve docs they're allowed to see).
Deliverable: Project 2 v1.0 deployed — chat UI with citations that link to source spans, per-answer faithfulness score, monitoring dashboard (latency p50/p95, tokens, cost, retrieval hit rate), eval suite runnable in CI.
DSA: Topological sort, union-find (12 problems) + review.
Gate: I inject an instruction into one of your documents. Your system must not follow it, and you must show me the trace.
Capstone: Phase B Final: "RAG on a new domain in a day": take a public dataset (e.g. a government policy PDF set), stand up an evaluated, cited RAG system by end of day. Write the trade-offs you'd change with a week.
Phase C — ML for AI Engineers + Agentic Systems (Months 8–10)
Month 8 — Applied ML on telecom signals

Learn: Statistics and probability for engineers (distributions, mean/variance, percentiles, confidence intervals, correlation vs causation, base rates), NumPy/Pandas fluency, sklearn pipeline: train/val/test, cross-validation, over/underfitting, regression, classification, trees, random forest, gradient boosting, metrics (precision/recall/F1/ROC-AUC/calibration), feature engineering, model interpretation (feature importance, SHAP intuition), anomaly detection (z-score, isolation forest, seasonal decomposition, simple forecasting residuals).
Deliverable: Project 1 gains ML anomaly detection on synthetic traffic/KPI time series (call attempts, success rates, latency), replacing the threshold detector, with a comparison of the two approaches and a model card.
DSA: Dynamic programming basics (16 problems).
Gate: Given a new synthetic KPI dataset, produce a validated anomaly detector with an honest metrics report in one sitting.
Capstone: Explain to a non-ML manager why your detector's false positives went up after a holiday and what you did.
Month 9 — Agents, state, LangGraph

Learn: The spectrum (chatbot → LLM app → RAG app → tool-using LLM → agent → multi-agent), agent loops, tool selection, planning vs reacting, state as a first-class object, short/long-term memory, routing, human-in-the-loop interrupts, checkpointing/resumability, failure handling (tool errors, loops, budget limits), agent evaluation (task success, trajectory scoring, cost per task).
Deliverable: Project 3 v0.5 — Incident Assistant agent: given an alert, it pulls related tickets (Project 1 API), retrieves runbooks (Project 2), summarizes, proposes root cause and actions, and stops for human approval before any "action" tool (all actions are simulated). Built in LangGraph with persisted state.
DSA: Mixed review, intermediate problems (16).
Gate: Kill the process mid-run; the agent must resume from its checkpoint. Show the trajectory trace.
Capstone: Write the agent eval set (30 incidents with expected actions) and report task success rate.
Month 10 — MCP, agent security, observability, production

Learn: MCP architecture (host/client/server, tools, resources, prompts, transports stdio/HTTP, auth), building an MCP server that exposes your Ops Platform tools, connecting your agent and a desktop client to it, when MCP is useful vs. plain function calling. Security: least-privilege tools, allow-lists, input/output validation, prompt injection via tool results, secrets handling, audit logging of every tool call, rate/budget limits. Observability: traces per run, token/cost per step, alerting on failures.
Deliverable: Project 3 v1.0 deployed — MCP server + LangGraph agent + approval UI + audit log + tracing dashboard + security write-up.
DSA: Mock-interview-style timed sets (2 × 45 min per week).
Gate: Red-team your own agent for an hour with me; every finding gets a fix and a test.
Capstone: Phase C Final: record a 10-minute architecture walkthrough video of Project 3 as if presenting to a customer's engineering team (forward-deployed muscle).
Phase D — System Design, Positioning, Interviews (Months 11–12)
Month 11 — System design + portfolio polish + applications begin

Learn: The 7 canonical designs (AI support assistant, enterprise RAG platform, agent platform, telecom monitoring platform, notification/alerting system, document processing system, AI ticket classification) each through: requirements → architecture → APIs → data → caching → queues → scaling → security → observability → failure → AI risks → cost → latency → trade-offs. One design per 2–3 days, written and diagrammed. Spring Boot refresh: one weekend, one small REST service, so the resume line is honest.
Deliverable: Resume v1, LinkedIn rewritten, GitHub profile README, three project READMEs with diagrams and "what I'd do differently". Start applying (10–15 targeted applications/week).
DSA: Timed mocks; revisit weak patterns.
Gate: Whiteboard any of the 7 designs cold in 40 minutes with trade-offs.
Month 12 — Interview sprint + Final Challenge

Weekly: 2 DSA mocks, 1 system design mock, 1 project deep-dive mock, behavioral prep (STAR stories from support work), SQL drills, Python/FastAPI rapid-fire.
Final AI Engineer Challenge (one week, ~20 hours): A new business brief arrives: "A mid-size ISP wants an AI assistant that watches network alerts, correlates them with customer complaints, drafts incident summaries with cited runbook steps, and proposes remediation for engineer approval. Deliver a design doc, working prototype, eval report, security review, and a 15-minute presentation." You must use Python, FastAPI, PostgreSQL (+pgvector), React, Docker, cloud deploy, an ML anomaly signal, LLM structured outputs, RAG with citations, a LangGraph agent with human approval, an MCP tool server, an eval suite, and observability. If you can do this, you are the engineer you set out to become.
