Part 3 — The First 30 Days in Detail
Daily routine (weekdays, ~2.5h)
20 min: DSA (one problem, or finish yesterday's)
60–90 min: the day's block (theory Mon/Tue, coding Wed/Thu, project Fri)
15 min: log what you built and one thing you don't understand yet (keep a LEARNING_LOG.md in your repo)
Weekend (~7–8h total)
Saturday 4h: project
Sunday 3–4h: project + review + one DSA re-solve + read one piece of official documentation end to end
Week 1 — Python like a professional + Git
Learn (theory ~4h): project layout (src/ layout, pyproject.toml), uv or venv + pip, type hints (Optional, list[str], TypedDict, Protocol), dataclasses, exception design (custom hierarchies, raise ... from, never bare except), logging (loggers, handlers, formatters, why not print), pytest (fixtures, parametrize, tmp_path), Git branching, meaningful commits, PRs to yourself.

Concepts to understand deeply: mutability and aliasing; when to use a dataclass vs dict vs Pydantic model; exception vs error code; why logging config lives at the entry point only.

Coding exercises:

ticketparse — a CLI that reads a JSONL file of synthetic tickets, validates each record, logs bad rows with reasons, and writes clean output. Typed, tested, packaged, installable with pip install -e ..
Write 5 custom exceptions for a fake payments module and a function that translates them into user-facing messages.
DSA (4): Two Sum, Valid Anagram, Contains Duplicate, Group Anagrams.

Real-world task: Read the Python logging HOWTO and the pytest "Getting Started" and "Fixtures" pages fully. Set up ruff and mypy in your repo; get to zero warnings.

Common mistakes: giant single-file scripts; from x import *; catching Exception and moving on; committing venv/; using print for anything not user-facing.

Resources: Python official tutorial (chapters 8–10), logging HOWTO, pytest docs, Fluent Python ch. 1–5 (skim), Pro Git ch. 2–3.

Checklist: [ ] ticketparse passes 10+ tests [ ] ruff + mypy clean [ ] you can explain raise ... from [ ] 4 DSA solved and re-explained aloud

Week 2 — Deeper Python + SQL foundations
Learn: OOP the Python way (composition over inheritance, __repr__, @property, ABCs and Protocol), iterators/generators (yield, lazy pipelines), decorators (with functools.wraps; write a @retry and a @timed), context managers (with, contextlib), JSON/file handling, pathlib, comprehensions vs loops. SQL: SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, JOIN (inner/left), GROUP BY/HAVING, basic aggregates, CREATE TABLE, primary/foreign keys, NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK.

Concepts to understand deeply: why generators matter for large log files; what a decorator actually does to the function object; how a foreign key protects data; the difference between WHERE and HAVING.

Coding exercises:

Generator pipeline: stream a 1-GB synthetic log file (generate it) and compute error rates per hour without loading it into memory.
@retry(times=3, backoff=2.0) decorator with tests using a fake clock.
Design (on paper, then SQL) tables for: users, tickets, ticket_comments, alerts. Load synthetic rows with a Python script using psycopg.
pgexercises.com: complete "Basic" and "Joins and Subqueries".
DSA (4): Top K Frequent Elements, Product of Array Except Self, Valid Palindrome, Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters (intro to sliding window).

Real-world task: run Postgres in Docker (docker run postgres:16), connect with psql, create your schema, write 10 questions your support team asks weekly ("tickets open > 48h by severity") as SQL queries.

Common mistakes: inheritance for code reuse; decorators that lose the function name; storing JSON blobs when you should have columns; no foreign keys "for flexibility".

Resources: Python docs on generators/functools/contextlib; PostgreSQL tutorial (ch. 2–3); pgexercises.com; Architecture Patterns with Python (cosmicpython.com, free) ch. 1–2.

Checklist: [ ] log pipeline handles 1 GB in constant memory [ ] @retry tested [ ] 4-table schema with constraints loads without errors [ ] 10 SQL queries saved in repo

Week 3 — FastAPI + Pydantic v2 + SQLAlchemy 2.0 + Alembic
Learn: HTTP (methods, status codes, idempotency, headers), REST resource design, FastAPI routing, path/query/body params, Pydantic v2 models (BaseModel, validators, model_config, separate request/response schemas), dependency injection (Depends, DB session per request), SQLAlchemy 2.0 ORM (declarative models, relationships, select() style, sessions), Alembic migrations (autogenerate, review, apply), settings via pydantic-settings + .env.

Concepts to understand deeply: why request and response schemas differ; what a session/transaction boundary is and where it should live; why migrations are code; what DI buys you in testing.

Coding exercises (this is Project 1 v0.1 starting):

POST/GET/PATCH /tickets, GET /tickets?status=&severity=&page= with proper 404/422 handling.
POST /alerts and GET /alerts with a relationship to tickets (an alert can be linked to a ticket).
Alembic migration history from empty DB to current schema.
Seeder CLI: python -m app.seed --tickets 5000 --alerts 20000 generating realistic synthetic data with Faker.
DSA (4): Valid Parentheses, Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock, Merge Intervals, 3Sum (two pointers).

Real-world task: read the FastAPI docs sections "Dependencies", "SQL Databases", "Handling Errors", and "Bigger Applications" fully. Read SQLAlchemy 2.0 "ORM Quick Start" and the Unified Tutorial's session section.

Common mistakes: returning ORM objects directly; one global session; PATCH that requires all fields; skipping Alembic and using create_all; logic in route handlers.

Resources: FastAPI docs, Pydantic v2 docs, SQLAlchemy 2.0 docs, Alembic tutorial, MDN HTTP overview.

Checklist: [ ] all endpoints in OpenAPI docs with examples [ ] migrations run clean from empty DB [ ] seeder produces 5k tickets in < 30s [ ] you can draw request → dependency → session → repository → DB

Week 4 — Testing, Docker Compose, layering, first CI
Learn: Test pyramid for APIs (unit tests for services, integration tests with a real test Postgres via Compose or testcontainers, TestClient/httpx.AsyncClient), factories for test data, separating routers / services / repositories / schemas / models, structured JSON logging with request IDs (middleware), health endpoint, Dockerfile for the API, Compose with api + db, GitHub Actions running lint + tests on every push.

Concepts to understand deeply: what to mock and what never to mock (don't mock your DB in integration tests); why a request ID exists; what happens in each layer of a multi-stage Docker build; why CI fails fast.

Coding exercises:

25+ tests: service unit tests, endpoint integration tests, a migration smoke test.
Middleware that adds X-Request-ID, logs method/path/status/duration as JSON.
docker compose up brings up API + Postgres, runs migrations, seeds data.
GitHub Actions workflow: ruff → mypy → pytest with a Postgres service container.
DSA (4): Binary Search, Reverse Linked List, Maximum Subarray, Min Stack.

Real-world task: Write the README: problem statement (generic telecom support), architecture diagram (Mermaid), how to run, decisions and trade-offs, SYNTHETIC_DATA.md.

Common mistakes: tests that depend on order; SQLite in tests but Postgres in prod; secrets in the image; latest tags; no .dockerignore.

Resources: FastAPI "Testing" docs; Docker "Multi-stage builds" and Compose docs; GitHub Actions "Quickstart" + "Using service containers"; 12-factor app (config, logs sections).

Checklist: [ ] CI green badge in README [ ] fresh clone runs in < 5 min [ ] logs are JSON with request IDs [ ] 25+ tests, integration tests use real Postgres

