Part 4 — First Project Milestone: Support Ops Platform v0.1 (end of Month 1)
Repo: telecom-support-ops-platform (public). Domain: a fictional operator "NorthStar Telecom". All data synthetic.

Scope of v0.1

Entities: users (no auth yet, just records), tickets (id, title, description, category, severity, status, created_by, assigned_to, created_at, updated_at, resolved_at), ticket_comments, alerts (id, source_system, metric, value, threshold, severity, fired_at, ticket_id nullable).
Endpoints: full ticket CRUD + filtering + pagination; alert create/list/link-to-ticket; GET /health; GET /stats/summary (counts by status/severity, done in SQL with GROUP BY).
Infra: Postgres 16 in Compose, Alembic migrations, seeder CLI, JSON logging with request IDs, 25+ tests, GitHub Actions CI, README with Mermaid diagram.
Explicitly not in v0.1: auth, React, background jobs, AI. Those are Months 2–5. Resist adding them.

Definition of done: a stranger can clone it, run one command, hit /docs, filter tickets, and read your README to understand every decision. You can explain every file.

Part 5 — Competency Gate: pass this before Month 2
Do these in one sitting (~4 hours), no Google, no AI assistants, official docs only, and record yourself explaining afterwards.

Build without tutorials (90 min): a new tiny FastAPI service oncall-roster with one table (shifts: engineer, start, end), POST/GET /shifts, a filter by date range, Pydantic validation that end > start, an Alembic migration, 5 tests including one integration test against Postgres, running in Compose. Ship it with a Dockerfile.
Explain on a whiteboard (15 min): draw the request lifecycle in Project 1 from HTTP → middleware → router → dependency → service → repository → SQLAlchemy session → Postgres → response, and where a transaction begins and ends.
Debug a broken API (30 min): I will give you a branch of your own repo with 3 injected bugs (a session leak, a wrong status code, a broken migration). Find and fix all three with tests that would have caught them.
SQL (30 min): on your seeded data, write: tickets per severity per week for the last 8 weeks; the 10 longest-open tickets with their latest comment; alerts that fired without a linked ticket, grouped by source system. Then add one index that measurably helps one of them and show EXPLAIN ANALYZE before/after.
Python (30 min): write a generator that streams JSONL, a @retry decorator with exponential backoff, and a context manager that times a block and logs it; all with type hints and tests.
DSA (30 min): solve two unseen Easy/Medium problems from Month 1 topics and state complexity.
Levels

Beginner: needed references for basic FastAPI/SQLAlchemy syntax; tests missing.
Competent: finished 1–5 with docs only; one or two gaps.
Production-ready: everything works, tested, logged, containerized, explainable.
Interview-ready: all of the above plus you answered the 10 Month-1 questions aloud in under 2 minutes each with a concrete example from your code.
You move to Month 2 at Competent or above. If you're at Beginner, spend one more week on the weakest area, then retake. This isn't punishment; every later month stacks on this one.

Part 6 — Career Positioning (start now, refine in Month 11)
Your story, one sentence: "I spent two years keeping a telecom charging platform running for real customers; I learned what breaks, what's repetitive, and what engineers actually need. Then I built the platform I wished we'd had, and taught it to think."

The progression you'll be able to prove: Support Engineer (understands incidents, SLAs, runbooks, escalation, on-call reality) → Automation (seeder, parsers, job workers in Project 1) → Backend Engineering (Project 1 v1.0) → AI/GenAI Applications (classification, RAG in Project 2) → AI Engineer (evaluated, monitored, secured agent in Project 3) → Applied / Forward-Deployed (Final Challenge: brief → design → build → present).

How to find problems worth automating (do this at work, keep it in a private notebook, never in public repos): each week, note one task that is repetitive, rule-based or knowledge-lookup-heavy, and error-prone under time pressure. Ask: what input, what decision, what output, who approves? That template is the exact spec for a tool, a job, a RAG query, or an agent step. Your projects reimplement those patterns on synthetic data, never the real thing.

GitHub strategy: three repos (Project 1, 2, 3) plus a synthetic-telecom-data package they all share. Every repo: badges, architecture diagram, "decisions and trade-offs", "what I'd do differently", eval results where relevant, 1–2 minute demo GIF. Commit history shows real work, not one giant "initial commit".

Resume strategy (Month 11): lead with a 3-line summary using the sentence above; projects section before experience; each project = problem → architecture → measurable result (e.g. "faithfulness 0.91 on 200-question eval, p95 latency 2.1s, $0.004/query"). Support role bullets reframed as outcomes ("reduced repeat escalations by scripting X" is fine if true and non-confidential; otherwise describe skills, not specifics).

Application strategy (Months 11–12): 10–15 targeted roles/week; prioritize companies building AI products for enterprises/telecom/support, mid-size firms with "Applied AI" or "Forward Deployed" titles, and AI-forward consultancies; referrals through LinkedIn posts about your project learnings (one post every two weeks from Month 5, technical and specific, no fluff).

